<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achaemenid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Achaemenids, first Persian Empire, Achaemenid dynasty, Achaemenid Persian Empire, Achaemenid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Achaemenid Empire was the first Persian Empire, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> in the sixth century BCE and stretching at its height from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. It governed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, royal roads and a policy of tolerating local customs and religions. It absorbed the Median, Babylonian and Egyptian realms and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in the 330s BCE, influencing later states such as the Seleucid and Sasanian empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire.jpg" alt="Achaemenid Empire" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cattette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achaemenid_Empire_500_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Third Dynasty of Ur (c. 2112–2004 BCE), the Old Babylonian kingdom, the Assyrian Empire and ultimately the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> all drew on administrative, ideological and cultural precedents set in the Akkadian period, and later Mesopotamian kings revered Sargon and Naram-Sin as models of kingship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It passed under the Chaldean Empire and from 539 BCE under the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b>, when it minted its own silver coins showing warships and was home to a Persian-period fortress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Cyrus the Great conquered Lydia in 546 BCE, Chios, according to Herodotus, submitted to the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> of its own accord, although the Persians as yet had no fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Cyrus the Great took Babylon in 539 BCE Dilmun passed to the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b>, and it disappears from the record as a political entity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cyrus the Great's absorption of Elam into the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> in the 540s and 530s BCE ended political independence, yet Susa was soon elevated by Darius I into a principal Persian royal capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Achaemenid Empire</b> the city paid tribute through the satrap at Sardis and was governed by local leaders acceptable to Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
